  "items": [
    {
      "id": "def-qftx-adhm-linear-data-stability-and-regularity",
      "kind": "definition",
      "title": "ADHM linear data, stability, costability and FI levels",
      "statement": "Fix integers $N\\ge1$ and $k\\ge0$ and standard Hermitian spaces $V=\\mathbb C^k$, $W=\\mathbb C^N$. A framed ADHM linear datum is $x=(B_1,B_2,I,J)$ with $B_i\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$, $I:W\\to V$ and $J:V\\to W$. Define the complex and real polynomial expressions\n\n$$\\mu_{\\mathbb C}(x)=[B_1,B_2]+IJ,\\qquad \\mu_{\\mathbb R}(x)=[B_1,B_1^\\dagger]+[B_2,B_2^\\dagger]+II^\\dagger-J^\\dagger J.$$\n\nThe datum is stable if the vectors $B_{i_1}\\cdots B_{i_m}I(w)$, with $m\\ge0$, $i_j\\in\\{1,2\\}$ and $w\\in W$, span $V$. It is costable if there is no nonzero subspace of $V$ invariant under both $B_i$ and contained in $\\ker J$. It is regular if it is stable and costable. The group $GL(V)$ acts by $g\\cdot x=(gB_1g^{-1},gB_2g^{-1},gI,Jg^{-1})$. Its unitary subgroup is $U(V)$.\n\nWrite $X^s=\\{x:\\mu_{\\mathbb C}(x)=0,\\ x\\text{ stable}\\}$, $X^{\\mathrm{reg}}=\\{x\\in X^s:x\\text{ costable}\\}$ and, for real $\\zeta\\ge0$, $X_\\zeta^U=\\{x\\in X^s:\\mu_{\\mathbb R}(x)=\\zeta I_V\\}$. Denote the corresponding orbit sets by $X^s/GL(V)$, $X^{\\mathrm{reg}}/GL(V)$ and $X_\\zeta^U/U(V)$, with quotient topologies when discussing their analytic spaces. A word frame consists of $k$ columns obtained by fixed words in $B_1,B_2$ applied to fixed standard basis vectors of $W$.\n\nFor $T_{\\mathbb C}=(\\mathbb C^*)^2\\times(\\mathbb C^*)^N$, write $t_1,t_2,e_1,\\ldots,e_N$ for the coordinate characters and put $h=\\operatorname{diag}(e_1^{-1},\\ldots,e_N^{-1})$. Its action is $(t_1,t_2,h)\\cdot x=(t_1B_1,t_2B_2,Ih^{-1},t_1t_2hJ)$. The compact torus is its subgroup on which all characters have modulus one. The framing representation thus has character $\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha^{-1}$ and its dual has character $\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha$.\n\nThese are finite-dimensional matrix definitions. They do not assert a real/complex quotient correspondence, a sheaf or ASD identification, or a noncompact pushforward. In particular, positive-FI stability and zero-FI regularity are distinct conditions; neither an endpoint identification nor an ordinary ASD interpretation follows from this definition.",
      "deps": [],
      "sources": {
        "references": [
          {
            "title": "Nikita A. Nekrasov, Seiberg–Witten Prepotential from Instanton Counting",
            "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0206161"
          },
          {
            "title": "Hiraku Nakajima and Kota Yoshioka, Lectures on Instanton Counting",
            "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0311058"
          }
        ]
      },
      "strategy": "",
      "proof_strategy": "definition"
    },
    {
      "id": "lem-adhm-stable-quotient-and-torus-lift",
      "kind": "lemma",
      "title": "Stable ADHM quotient charts, properness and torus lifts",
      "statement": "Assume AC. Fix $N\\ge1$, $k\\ge0$, $V=\\mathbb C^k$ and $W=\\mathbb C^N$ with their standard bases. Let $X$ be the stable locus in the complex ADHM equation $[B_1,B_2]+IJ=0$, where $B_i\\in\\operatorname{End}(V)$, $I:W\\to V$, $J:V\\to W$, and stability means that the vectors $B_{i_1}\\cdots B_{i_m}I(w)$ span $V$. The action $g\\cdot(B_1,B_2,I,J)=(gB_1g^{-1},gB_2g^{-1},gI,Jg^{-1})$ of $GL(V)$ on $X$ is free and proper. Its orbit quotient with quotient topology is a Hausdorff second-countable complex manifold of complex dimension $2kN$, with tangent space $\\ker d_1/\\operatorname{im}d_0$ for the infinitesimal ADHM deformation maps.\n\nFor $k>0$, a word frame $C(x)$ is a matrix whose columns are $k$ fixed words in $B_1,B_2$ applied to fixed standard framing basis vectors. The opens $U_C=\\{x:\\det C(x)\\ne0\\}$ cover $X$; words of length at most $k-1$ suffice. Each slice $S_C=\\{x\\in U_C:C(x)=I_V\\}$ gives quotient coordinates, with $GL(V)\\times S_C\\to U_C$ a biholomorphism. Slice changes are holomorphic, in fact given by matrix inversion and polynomial operations on their domains. The quotient is also a separated smooth finite-type complex algebraic variety with a finite $T$-invariant affine atlas of these slices, and the map from $X$ is a geometric quotient in the regular-function sense.\n\nLet $T=(S^1)^2\\times(S^1)^N$ act by $(t_1,t_2,h)\\cdot(B_1,B_2,I,J)=(t_1B_1,t_2B_2,Ih^{-1},t_1t_2hJ)$, with $h$ acting on the $\\alpha$th standard framing vector by $e_\\alpha^{-1}$. This descends to a smooth holomorphic action. At any fixed quotient point represented by $x$, there is a unique $g(t)\\in GL(V)$ with $t\\cdot x=g(t)\\cdot x$. If column $\\ell$ of $C(x)$ uses framing color $\\alpha_\\ell$ and contains $a_\\ell,b_\\ell$ letters $B_1,B_2$, then\n\n$$g(t)=C(x)\\operatorname{diag}(e_{\\alpha_\\ell}t_1^{a_\\ell}t_2^{b_\\ell})C(x)^{-1}.$$\n\nThus the lift is a smooth character representation, without assuming Zariski density or a GIT linearization theorem. For $k=0$ the quotient is a point and these matrices and tangent spaces have their empty-dimensional interpretations. No correspondence with a real moment-map quotient, sheaf moduli or ASD connections is asserted.",
      "deps": [
        "def-qftx-adhm-linear-data-stability-and-regularity",
